**Key points**

1. `model()` can return a *hash* (`RSVP.hash`) that resolves when all promises inside it resolve.  
2. The hash keys (`users`, `posts`) become properties of the model object.  
3. `setupController` is optional; it lets you expose each part of the hash as a separate controller property for cleaner template syntax.  
4. In the template you can iterate over each collection with `{{#each this.users}}` and `{{#each this.posts}}`.

```js
// app/routes/index.js
import Route from '@ember/routing/route';
import { hash } from 'rsvp';

export default class IndexRoute extends Route {
  async model() {
    // Load two unrelated models in parallel
    return hash({
      users: this.store.findAll('user'),
      posts: this.store.findAll('post')
    });
  }
}
```

```hbs
<!-- app/templates/index.hbs -->
<h2>Users</h2>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Name</th><th>Email</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    {{#each this.model.users as |user|}}
      <tr>
        <td>{{user.name}}</td>
        <td>{{user.email}}</td>
      </tr>
    {{/each}}
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>Posts</h2>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Title</th><th>Body</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    {{#each this.model.posts as |post|}}
      <tr>
        <td>{{post.title}}</td>
        <td>{{post.body}}</td>
      </tr>
    {{/each}}
  </tbody>
</table>
```

```js
// app/controllers/index.js   (optional – only if you want separate properties)
import Controller from '@ember/controller';

export default class IndexController extends Controller {
  get users() { return this.model.users; }
  get posts() { return this.model.posts; }
}
```

The route’s `model()` hook returns a hash of promises; Ember waits for all of them to resolve before rendering the template.  
In the template you can access each collection via `this.model.users` and `this.model.posts` (or via the controller properties if you use the controller shown above).

