The component sends `input` to the controller via the `onCompute` closure action.  
The controller computes a value, stores it in the tracked `result` property, and the component receives that value back through the `@result` argument.

```js
// app/controllers/my-controller.js
import Controller from '@ember/controller';
import { tracked } from '@glimmer/tracking';
import { action } from '@ember/object';

export default class MyController extends Controller {
  @tracked input = '';
  @tracked result = null;

  @action
  computeResult() {
    // Example computation – reverse the input string
    this.result = this.input.split('').reverse().join('');
  }
}
```

```hbs
<!-- app/templates/my-template.hbs -->
<MyComponent
  @input={{this.input}}
  @result={{this.result}}
  @compute={{this.computeResult}}
/>
```

```js
// app/components/my-component.js
import Component from '@glimmer/component';
import { action } from '@ember/object';

export default class MyComponent extends Component {
  @action
  handleClick() {
    // Call the action passed from the controller
    this.args.compute();
  }
}
```

```hbs
<!-- app/templates/components/my-component.hbs -->
<div>
  <input
    type="text"
    value={{this.args.input}}
    {{on "input" (fn (mut this.args.input) value="target.value")}}
  />

  <button {{on "click" this.handleClick}}>
    Compute
  </button>

  {{#if this.args.result}}
    <p>Result: {{this.args.result}}</p>
  {{/if}}
</div>
```

**How it works**

1. The controller holds the data (`input`, `result`) and the action (`computeResult`).
2. The template passes those values to the component via attributes (`@input`, `@result`, `@compute`).
3. The component can read the passed values (`this.args.input`, `this.args.result`) and invoke the action (`this.args.compute()`) when needed.

